Here's my maintainability analysis of `InputValidatorTests.cs`:

---

## Summary

**1 high-value pattern found** across 9 test methods. The suite is a textbook candidate for **parameterized tests** — every method has an identical body differing only in the input string and expected outcome.

---

## Finding: Copy-Paste Test Methods → Convert to `[DataTestMethod]`

**All 9 methods** share this structure:
```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(INPUT);
Assert.IsTrue/IsFalse(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual/IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
```

They split into two groups:

### Group A — Invalid inputs (5 methods, lines 8–78)
`Validate_EmptyString_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_TooShort_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_TooLong_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_ContainsSpecialChars_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_Null_ReturnsFalse`, `Validate_WhitespaceOnly_ReturnsFalse`

### Group B — Valid inputs (3 methods, lines 44–68)
`Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue`, `Validate_MinLength_ReturnsTrue`, `Validate_MaxLength_ReturnsTrue`

### Suggested refactoring — after:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("",         "Input cannot be empty",                  DisplayName = "Empty string")]
    [DataRow(null,       "Input cannot be empty",                  DisplayName = "Null")]
    [DataRow("   ",      "Input cannot be empty",                  DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
    [DataRow("ab",       "Input must be at least 3 characters",    DisplayName = "Too short")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    public void Validate_InvalidInput_ReturnsFalse(string? input, string expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.IsFalse(result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("hello-world", DisplayName = "Typical valid input")]
    [DataRow("abc",         DisplayName = "Min length (3)")]
    public void Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsTrue(string input)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.IsTrue(result.IsValid);
        Assert.IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

> **Note:** The "too long" case (`new string('x', 256)`) can't be a `[DataRow]` because it's not a compile-time constant. Use `[DynamicData]` for it:

```csharp
    private static IEnumerable<object[]> TooLongInput =>
        [[ new string('x', 256), "Input must not exceed 255 characters" ]];

    [DataTestMethod]
    [DynamicData(nameof(TooLongInput))]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    // ... other DataRows ...
    public void Validate_InvalidInput_ReturnsFalse(string? input, string expectedError)
    { ... }
```

Similarly, `new string('a', 255)` for the valid max-length test needs `[DynamicData]`.

---

## Impact

| Metric | Before | After |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Test methods | 9 | 2 |
| Lines of code | ~80 | ~35 |
| Adding a new case | New 7-line method | One `[DataRow]` line |

## Trade-offs

| Gained | Lost |
|--------|------|
| Adding a test case = adding one line | Individual methods no longer visible as separate units in code |
| Less copy-paste risk | Slightly harder to debug a single failing case (mitigated by `DisplayName`) |
| Clear separation of valid vs. invalid | — |

**Priority: High** — this is low-risk, high-reward. The refactoring is mechanical and directly solves your "every case needs a whole new method" pain. Would you like me to apply these changes?
